MILD A Step-by-Step Speech Practice Guide For people who experience moderate stammering and want noticeable improvement. How to use this guide Practice calmly and consistently. The aim is not to force words out or speak unnaturally slowly. Use the exercises to develop relaxed breathing, controlled speech, comfortable vowel sounds, body awareness, and confidence in everyday conversations. This is a 9-month program with once-daily practice. TIMING & COMMITMENT: Practice schedule: Once daily • Session: 20-25 minutes at a time of day that works best for you • Morning recommended for fresh mind and positive tone setting • Sets foundation for daily interactions • With consistent practice, minimal interruptions needed Duration: 9 months of consistent practice (once daily) • Months 1-2: Exercises feel new, breathing becomes automatic • Months 3-4: Vocal exercises strengthen, body awareness develops • Months 5-6: Real-world improvements show, confidence builds • Months 7-9: Changes feel permanent, stammering significantly reduced The 9-month commitment with consistent once-daily practice is essential. Regular practice allows results to develop naturally and sustainably. By month 4, you will notice significant improvements. By month 9, stammering will be substantially reduced (50-70%). Important: This is an educational self-practice guide, not a medical treatment or guaranteed cure. If stammering is persistent, worsening, causing significant distress, or substantially interfering with school, work, or relationships, consider working with a qualified speech-language pathologist/speech therapist.Daily sequence — ONCE DAILY PRACTICE Practice one session daily for 20-25 minutes 1. Breathing (4 min) 20 slow nose breaths + 11 alternating-nostril repetitions 2. Vocal exercises (10 min) 15 words for each vowel sound, with 5–7 second vowel stretching 3. Mirror practice (5 min) Speak slowly in front of mirror. Observe body movements. Identify tension in jaw, shoulders, neck. Practice relaxation. 4. Slow reading (5 min) Read 4–5 words per comfortable mouth-inhale, using gentle vowel stretching 5. Subconscious rewiring (1–2 min) Focus on relaxation. Say: 'My voice is calm. My body is relaxed. I speak with confidence.' DURATION: Continue once daily for 9 months minimum • Consistency matters more than perfection • 20-25 minutes per day • Skip a session? Resume the next day. Do not skip two in a row. • Stick with it. Changes are significant and permanent after 9 months.STEP 1 — BREATHING EXERCISES The first goal is to make your breathing slow, steady, and relaxed before you begin speaking. A. Slow nasal breathing — 20 repetitions 1. Sit or stand comfortably and relax your shoulders. 2. Inhale slowly through your nose. 3. Exhale slowly through your nose. 4. Keep the breath comfortable. Do not force a deep breath. 5. Repeat 20 times. Practice cue: slow in → slow out → relaxed body. B. Alternating-nostril breathing — 11 repetitions 1. Close your left nostril gently with your finger. 2. Inhale slowly through your right nostril. 3. Release the left nostril and gently close the right nostril. 4. Exhale slowly through the left nostril. 5. Continue the pattern for 11 repetitions. 6. Keep the breathing comfortable rather than trying to take very large breaths. If you feel dizzy, uncomfortable, or short of breath, stop and return to normal breathing.STEP 2 — VOCAL EXERCISES Take a comfortable breath through your mouth, then gently stretch the target vowel for about 5–7 seconds before completing the word. Example: apple → aaaaaaaapple Do not squeeze your throat or push the voice. The stretch should feel gentle and controlled. Vowel A — 15 words aaaaaaapple, aaaaaaanimal, aaaaaaanswer, aaaaaaartist, aaaaaaafternoon, aaaaaaairport, aaaaaaactor, aaaaaaaddress, aaaaaaadventure, aaaaaaangel, aaaaaaapron, aaaaaaanchor, aaaaaaavocado, aaaaaaarrow, aaaaaaautumn Vowel E — 15 words eeeeeeegg, eeeeeeeleephant, eeeeeeenergy, eeeeeeengine, eeeeeeevening, eeeeeeeagle, eeeeeeearth, eeeeeeecho, eeeeeeelbow, eeeeeeeenvelope, eeeeeeexercise, eeeeeeentry, eeeeeeevent, eeeeeeexample, eeeeeeelevator Vowel I — 15 words iiiiiiice, iiiiiiidea, iiiiiiisland, iiiiiiimage, iiiiiiinside, iiiiiiinsect, iiiiiiinvite, iiiiiiimportant, iiiiiiinterest, iiiiiiinternet, iiiiiiinstant, iiiiiiincome, iiiiiiinjury, iiiiiiitem, iiiiiiimagine Vowel O — 15 words ooooooorange, ooooooocean, ooooooopen, oooooooffice, ooooooorder, ooooooonly, ooooooowner, oooooooasis, ooooooobject, oooooooutside, ooooooovertime, oooooooption, oooooooval, ooooooonion, oooooooriginal Vowel U — 15 words uuuuuuumbrella, uuuuuuuncle, uuuuuuunder, uuuuuuunit, uuuuuuuse, uuuuuuusual, uuuuuuuniverse, uuuuuuunique, uuuuuuuniform, uuuuuuup, uuuuuuupper, uuuuuuurgent, uuuuuuuseful, uuuuuuunderstand, uuuuuuupdatedSTEP 3 — MIRROR PRACTICE & BODY AWARENESS Stammering is not just about speech. It often involves tension in your body—jaw clenching, shoulder shrugging, neck tension, eye blinking patterns, head movements. Mirror practice helps you identify and eliminate these unwanted physical responses. Why mirror practice matters When you speak, you typically cannot see your own body. Mirror practice gives you real-time feedback on physical tension. As you notice these movements, you can consciously relax and eliminate them. This breaks the full-body stammer pattern—not just the voice stammer. How to practice (5 min daily) Setup: Stand in front of a mirror. Ensure good lighting so you can see your face and shoulders clearly. Step 1: Observe (1 min) 1. Look at yourself calmly in the mirror 2. Notice your baseline: Do you see tension? 3. Identify problem areas:- Jaw tension? (clenched teeth, tight jaw)- Shoulder tension? (shrugged shoulders, tightness)- Neck tension? (stiff neck, visible straining)- Facial tension? (furrowed brow, tight lips)- Eye movements? (excessive blinking, averted gaze)- Head movements? (head tilts, jerky motions) Step 2: Practice with awareness (3 min) 1. Speak slowly: Say your name, then a sentence: 'My name is [name]. I speak with calm and confidence.' 2. Watch yourself: As you speak, observe your body 3. Identify the moment tension appears: When do you clench? When do you tense? 4. Make note: Write down which body part shows most tension Step 3: Relax & eliminate (1 min) 1. Consciously relax the tense area: Relax jaw, drop shoulders, soften neck 2. Speak the same sentence again: 'My name is [name]. I speak with calm and confidence.' 3. Observe the difference: Is there less tension? Is speech smoother? 4. Practice 3 times: Each time, focus on keeping that body part relaxed What you're training• Awareness: Knowing when & where you tense up • Control: Consciously relaxing tense areas • Confidence: Speaking while relaxed feels better • Habit: After 9 months, relaxed speech becomes automatic Mirror practice checklist Each session, check off: Identified tense areas Consciously relaxed them Noticed improvement in fluency Wrote down which body part improved mostSTEP 4 — SLOW READING PRACTICE Now transfer the controlled breathing and vocal practice into connected speech. How to practice 1. Choose a short paragraph, story, article, or book. 2. Take a comfortable inhale through your mouth. 3. Read approximately 4–5 words on that breath. 4. Use the same gentle vowel-stretching technique while saying the words, aiming for approximately 5–6 seconds on the stretched vowel when practicing. 5. Pause, breathe comfortably, and continue. 6. Focus on smoothness and relaxation rather than speed. 7. If practicing in front of mirror, notice body tension and maintain relaxation Example rhythm Breathe → relax body → read 4–5 words slowly → pause → breathe → read the next 4–5 words. Important: The purpose is controlled practice, not permanently speaking every word with a long 5–6 second stretch. As the exercise becomes comfortable, speech should gradually become more natural.STEP 5 — SUBCONSCIOUS REWIRING Speech practice is not only about the mouth and breathing. You can also train your attention by deliberately noticing successful speaking experiences and replacing old negative expectations with new, realistic experiences. Daily process 1. Complete the breathing, vocal, mirror, and reading exercises (daily). 2. Speak with a friend or family member in a normal, relaxed conversation (during the day). 3. Do not obsessively monitor every word. Focus on communicating your message. 4. If you notice a conversation in which you spoke comfortably or experienced less stammering, write it down in your journal. 5. Record what happened: Who did I speak with? What did I talk about? What felt easier? Which body part stayed relaxed? 6. Read your successful moments every night before sleeping. Subconscious thought framework Use this framework as a reminder: "I am eliminating the hold that my past bad speaking experiences have had on me. I am creating new, positive speaking experiences by learning and practicing a correct, structured path. Every successful conversation gives my mind a new experience to learn from. My body stays relaxed. My voice is calm. I do not need to erase my past; I can learn from it and build a new pattern through consistent practice." Journal example "Today I spoke with my boss about a project. I noticed my shoulders started to tense up, but I took a breath and relaxed them. I spoke clearly and confidently. The conversation went well. My jaw stayed relaxed the entire time." The goal of the journal is to collect real experiences—not to pretend that every conversation will be perfectly fluent.STEP 6 — NIGHT-TIME POSITIVE FRAMEWORK Before sleeping, read your journal and repeat a supportive statement. Keep the statement realistic and focused on your actions and progress. Choose one or more: • "I am doing my part to improve my speech and body control, one step at a time." • "I am practicing consistently (once daily) and becoming more comfortable with speaking." • "I do not need to rush my words. My body is relaxed. I communicate calmly." • "I am learning a better path to speaking and I am practicing it every day." • "My body is learning to relax when I speak. My tension is decreasing. My confidence is increasing." • "My past experiences do not have to determine every future conversation. I am creating new experiences through practice." Night routine: Read your journal → breathe comfortably → repeat your chosen framework → sleep.STEP 7 — THE 9-MONTH COMMITMENT ROADMAP Months 1–2: Foundation & Body Awareness • Breathing becomes automatic • Vocal exercises feel less awkward • Mirror practice reveals body tension patterns • You identify where you hold stress (jaw, shoulders, neck) • Goal: Understand your physical stammer pattern Months 3–4: Vocal Strength & Relaxation • Speech feels smoother when reading aloud • Vocal exercises strengthen your voice • Mirror practice shows decreasing tension • You can consciously relax tense areas • Goal: Body-relaxed speech becomes easier Months 5–6: Real-World Integration • Noticeable reduction in stammering (40–50%) • Comfortable speaking in small group conversations • Body tension has significantly decreased • Less anxiety before speaking • Goal: Stammering is not dominating your life Months 7–9: Permanence & Confidence • Stammering is significantly reduced (50–70%) • Confidence in most speaking situations • Your body stays naturally relaxed when speaking • Mirror practice shows smooth, tension-free speech • Goal: Changes feel permanent and automatic What to expect: Progress is not linear. Some days will be easier than others. Stress may temporarily increase stammering. Use breathing exercises. By month 4, you will see significant body-relaxation improvement. By month 9, the changes are yours to keep. Stammering is substantially reduced. Keep going. The 9-month consistent investment (once daily) pays massive dividends for life.STEP 8 — RECOMMENDED VIDEOS These resources are included as supplementary learning. Watch them to learn communication, confidence, mindset, body language, and the lived experience of stammering. They are not substitutes for individualized speech therapy. Speaking & confidence 1. Julian Treasure — "How to Speak So That People Want to Listen" Covers vocal exercises, powerful speaking, body language, and empathy. 2. Amy Cuddy — "Your Body Language May Shape Who You Are" Explores how posture and body language affect confidence and success. 3. Celeste Headlee — "10 Ways to Have a Better Conversation" Practical principles for authentic conversations and connection. 4. Manish Upadhyay — "A Journey From Stutter to Trainer" Personal experience of overcoming stammering and building confidence. 5. LeRon Barton — "How I Overcame My Fear of Stuttering" Focuses on fear elimination and speaking with confidence. Mind training & mental practice 6. Andy Puddicombe — "All It Takes Is 10 Mindful Minutes" Short mindfulness practice for awareness and body connection. 7. TED playlist — "Best TED Talks on Mindset and Positive Thinking" Covers mindfulness, stress, resilience, and mental habits. 8. Dr. Jeetendra Adhia — "Unleash the Power of Subconscious Mind" Explores how to reprogram subconscious patterns. Suggested viewing order: Julian Treasure → Amy Cuddy → Manish Upadhyay → LeRon Barton → Celeste Headlee → Andy Puddicombe. Final reminder The objective is not to demand perfect fluency. Build a calmer, more relaxed relationship with speaking. Practice consistently (once daily). Notice real improvements in your body and voice. Continue communicating even when speech is not perfect. Your body will thank you.